    "created_at": "2016-11-20T22:41:12Z",
    "updated_at": "2016-11-20T22:41:12Z",
    "body": "```\nSELECT url FROM (\n  SELECT\n    url,\n    JSON_EXTRACT(payload, '$._doctype') AS doctype\n  FROM [httparchive:har.2016_11_01_chrome_pages]\n)\nWHERE doctype = \"\\\"html about:legacy-compat\\\"\"\n```\n\n123 pages (out of 165,162, so ~0.07%).\n\nThe stat from https://validator.w3.org/nu/stats.html is currently\n\n> Doctype with「SYSTEM \"about:legacy-compat\"」found   65  0.000356\n\n(which is about the same amount as \"Detected language: Norwegian\")\n\nhttp://stackoverflow.com/search?q=about%3Alegacy-compat shows 149 results, and not only XSLT but also Java DOM, Scala XML, etc.\n\nI haven't seen tutorials even mentioning this, so it's not really the case that \"all HTML authors\" are burdened by it existing. See for example\n- https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/Guide/HTML/HTML5/Introduction_to_HTML5\n- https://www.w3.org/QA/2002/04/valid-dtd-list.html\n\nSo I think we should not make this change. The doctype serves a purpose and people are using it. Removing it would mostly be for aesthetic reasons.\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
